#: ../../../performance.rst:2
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:4
msgid "This page describes how to use psutil efficiently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:9
msgid "Use oneshot() when reading multiple process attributes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:11
msgid ""
"If you're dealing with a single :class:`Process` instance and need to "
"retrieve multiple process attributes, use :meth:`Process.oneshot`. Each "
"method call issues a separate system call, but the OS often returns multiple "
"attributes at once, which :meth:`Process.oneshot` caches for subsequent "
"calls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:16 ../../../performance.rst:56
msgid "Slow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:18
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"p = psutil.Process()\n"
"p.name()           # syscall\n"
"p.cpu_times()      # syscall\n"
"p.memory_info()    # syscall\n"
"p.status()         # syscall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:28 ../../../performance.rst:68
msgid "Fast:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:30
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"p = psutil.Process()\n"
"with p.oneshot():\n"
"    p.name()         # one syscall, result cached\n"
"    p.cpu_times()    # from cache\n"
"    p.memory_info()  # from cache\n"
"    p.status()       # from cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:41
msgid ""
"The speed improvement depends on the platform and on how many attributes you "
"read. On Linux the gain is typically around 1.5x–2x; on Windows it can be "
"much higher. As a rule of thumb: if you read more than one attribute from "
"the same process, use :meth:`Process.oneshot`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:49
msgid "Use process_iter() with an attrs list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:51
msgid ""
"If you iterate over multiple PIDs, always use :func:`process_iter`. It "
"accepts an ``attrs`` argument that pre-fetches only the requested attributes "
"in a single pass, minimizing system calls by fetching multiple attributes at "
"once. This is faster than calling individual methods in a loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:58
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for p in psutil.process_iter():\n"
"    try:\n"
"        print(p.pid, p.name(), p.status())\n"
"    except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):\n"
"        pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:70
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for p in psutil.process_iter([\"name\", \"status\"]):\n"
"    print(p.pid, p.name(), p.status())  # return cached values, never raise"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:77
msgid ""
":func:`process_iter(attrs=...) <psutil.process_iter>` is effectively "
"equivalent to using :meth:`Process.oneshot` on each process. Using :func:"
"`process_iter` also saves you from **race conditions** (e.g. if a process "
"disappears while iterating), since :exc:`NoSuchProcess` and :exc:"
"`AccessDenied` exceptions are handled internally. A typical use case is to "
"fetch all process attrs except the slow ones (see :ref:`perf-api-speed` "
"table below):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:84
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for p in psutil.process_iter(psutil.Process.attrs - {\"memory_footprint\", "
"\"memory_maps\"}):\n"
"    ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:94
msgid "Methods sped up by oneshot()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:96
msgid ""
"Here's a list of method groups for each platform which can benefit from :"
"meth:`Process.oneshot`. Methods in each group (in the same comma-separated "
"list) share the same underlying system call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:100
msgid ""
"The *speedup* represents the estimated gain when all listed methods are "
"called together (best case), as measured by :src:`scripts/internal/"
"bench_oneshot.py`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../performance.rst:103
msgid ""
"Additionally, some methods are computed from other methods, so on every "
"platform :meth:`Process.oneshot` also speeds up :meth:`~Process.cpu_percent` "
"(from :meth:`~Process.cpu_times`), :meth:`~Process.memory_percent` (from :"
"meth:`~Process.memory_info`), :meth:`~Process.parent` and :meth:`~Process."
"parents` (from :meth:`~Process.ppid`) and, on POSIX, :meth:`~Process."
"username` (from :meth:`~Process.uids`)."
msgstr ""

